{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>They have turned aside quickly.</b> The reason for 'quickly' is that they forgot those wonders which their eyes saw at the holy assembly, and served idolatry while the Shekhinah was still on Mount Sinai, in the manner of what is written (Song of Songs 1:12): ״עד שהמלך במסבו נרדי נתן ריחו״ — 'While the King was at His table, my nard gave forth its fragrance.' And it is the same expression that David said (Psalms 106:13): ״מהרו שכחו מעשיו לא חכו לעצתו״ — 'They made haste, they forgot His deeds; they did not wait for His counsel' — that they did not wait for His counsel until the morrow, for the Torah, which is called 'counsel,' as it is said (Isaiah 25:1): ״עצות מרחוק״ and so forth." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> סרו מהר מן הדרך — 'They turned aside quickly from the way' — a contemptible bride who committed adultery within her canopy. And thus it says: ״עד שהמלך במסבו נרדי נתן ריחו״ — while the King of the kings of kings was at His table, my nard gave forth its fragrance — it gave forth a foul fragrance through the deed of the calf." "<b>They made themselves a molten calf and worshipped it.</b> One must wonder in this pericah about several questions. The first: Israel, who were like the ministering angels and heard the Ten Commandments at Sinai from the mouth of Elohim — how could they ask Aaron to make them idolatry? And furthermore, how is it possible that they were utter simpletons, thinking that the gold jewelry that was in their ears was that which they had brought up from Egypt, concerning which they said, ״אלה אלהיך ישראל״ and so forth? And furthermore, Aaron, who was the chosen of HaShem — there was none in all Israel as great as he after Moses our master — how did he take the matter so lightly and not surrender himself to death, when concerning idolatry one is obligated to be killed rather than transgress? And furthermore, one must wonder about Aaron: how did Aaron reply to Moses, ״אל יחר אף אדוני״ — 'Let not the anger of my lord grow hot' — and with what is it fitting for a person to grow angry and pour out the wrath of his fury, if not over a grave transgression such as this?" "<b>And the answer.</b> It is that the intention of Israel in the matter of the calf was not to make it idolatry, Heaven forbid — for they did not ask for it as a deity, but only as a leader. This is what they said: ״אשר ילכו לפנינו״ — 'that shall go before us' — that it should be our leader and show us the way through the wilderness. For Moses had been lost from them, and the journeys were made by his direction, so they sought a replacement for Moses who would show them the way. And this is what he explained: ״כי זה משה האיש אשר העלנו״ and so forth — he gave the reason for their request: that Moses, who was their leader, had been lost from them, and they did not know what had happened to him. And it is obvious that Israel did not consider Moses to be a god to Israel, such that they would say 'make us a god' because 'the god of ours has been lost from us'; rather, just as Moses was a leader, so they sought another leader in place of Moses who had been lost from them. And the reason Aaron involved himself in its making was that his intention was to delay them with it, so that in the meantime Moses would come and nullify the matter; and he did not wish to surrender himself to death, for he was not obligated to do so since they had not asked him for the sake of idolatry. And what proves this — that their intention was not idolatry, and all the more so the intention of Aaron — is what Aaron replied to Moses when he said to him, ״אל יחר אף אדוני״ — that is, do not be so angry, for they did not ask me for idolatry but asked for a leader in your place. And this is what he added, saying: ״ויאמרו לי עשה לנו אלהים אשר ילכו לפנינו כי זה משה האיש״ — that is, because you, my lord, were delayed, in their view, they asked me to make them a leader who would show them the way; and if you, my lord, had come in the meantime, they would immediately abandon it and follow you as at first. And this is why you will not find in the pericah that it mentions ״קום עשה לנו אלהים״ without immediately mentioning ״כי זה משה האיש״ — he explained the reason for the request: that their request for a 'god' was only because of the absence of this Moses. And from here you have proof that their intention was not idolatry — how much more so the intention of Aaron. And this is why he said, ״חג לה' מחר״ — 'A feast to YHWH tomorrow' — and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ״חג לעגל״ — 'a feast to the calf' — is not written here, but rather ״חג לה'״ — 'a feast to YHWH' — to say that his intention was only for the designated Name. And the great proof that they did not ask for it for the sake of idolatry is that when Moses came down from the mountain and burned the calf, they let him burn it; had their intention been idolatry when they asked for it, how could they have let him burn it — would they let the abomination of theirs be burned before their eyes and not stone him? So explained the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>And you should ask.</b> here: since their intention in asking for the calf was not for any idolatry at all, why were they punished so severely — with killing at the hands of the Levites, as it is said, ״שימו איש חרבו על ירכו״ and so forth, and with a plague at the hands of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said, ״ויגוף ה' את העם״? And the answer: the matter of the calf began unwittingly and ended deliberately, as it is written, ״וישתחוו לו ויזבחו לו״ — 'and they worshipped it and sacrificed to it.' And Israel were divided into factions: some whose intention was for good, as from what is written, ״ויעלו עלות ויגישו שלמים״ — for it does not say ״ויעלו לו ויגישו לו״ — 'they offered to it and brought to it'; and some whose intention was for evil, as from what is written, ״וישתחוו לו ויזבחו לו״ — 'they worshipped it and sacrificed to it.'" "<b>And it is explicitly stated.</b> They said in the Midrash that the mixed multitude who went out with them from Egypt made it, and the verse proves this, for it says ״אלה אלהיך ישראל״ — 'these are your gods, O Israel' — and this is the language of the mixed multitude, who said to Israel, ״אלה אלהיך ישראל״ — 'these are your gods, O Israel'; for if Israel had made it, it should have said ״אלה אלהינו ישראל״ — 'these are our gods, O Israel' — ״אשר העלונו״ — 'that brought us up.' And this is what they said: converts are as harsh to Israel as a plague-spot on living flesh. And further, they said in the Midrash that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: לך רד כי שחת עמך — 'go down, for your people has corrupted' — עמך, yours and not Mine; for I did not say to you at the exodus from Egypt, ״והוצאת את צבאותי את עמי״ — 'and you shall bring out My hosts, My people' — but you accepted the mixed multitude, and you wanted to accept the converts; עמך הם, they are yours and not Mine. Nevertheless, even though the mixed multitude were initially the principal actors in the evil deed, we find that Israel went astray after it and all were punished."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:34.798454011Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}